{#
  _env_axis.j2 — the SINGLE renderer for the per-service env passthrough axis.

  Every compose template that honors a service's `env:` key imports this macro
  rather than writing the loop itself, for the same reason `_network_axis.j2`
  exists beside it: one axis, one renderer, so a set of templates cannot drift
  into a set of dialects that each spell "hand this host variable to the
  container" slightly differently.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IMPORTING
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The macro takes everything it needs as parameters, so a plain (context-free)
  import is correct — never `with context`:

    Service templates, rendered by an Environment rooted at the PROJECT root
    (deployment/compose_generator.py's `FileSystemLoader(".")`, template paths
    like `services/<name>/docker-compose.yml.j2`):

      {% import "services/_env_axis.j2" as hostenv %}

    Templates rendered by an Environment rooted at this directory import by
    bare name instead:

      {% import "_env_axis.j2" as hostenv %}

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE `svc` ARGUMENT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The macro takes the service's own config block as its first argument — the
  `services.<name>` mapping of the rendered config.yml:

      {{- hostenv.passthrough(services.qmd) }}

  It may arrive as a mapping (the usual case), as None, or undefined; the last
  two mean "the service declares nothing", which renders nothing at all. The
  one key read here is `env`: a list of host environment variable NAMES, in the
  author's order, which is the order they are emitted in.

  Names are validated where the profile is parsed — `env_names_errors` in
  cli/build_profile_schema.py, over both the nested `services.<name>.config.env`
  spelling and the dotted `services.<name>.env` one — and `ServiceDef.env_names()`
  is where the empty default is spelled for every reader on the Python side.
  Nothing malformed therefore reaches a render; a macro that somehow saw a bare
  string or a number where a list belongs emits nothing, rather than one line
  per character.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A BARE `${VAR}`: NO `:-` DEFAULT, NO `:?` REFUSAL
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A `:-<literal>` default would mean the macro inventing a value. It cannot: it
  knows a NAME and nothing whatever about what that name means to the service —
  a site proxy, a tuning knob, a path. A guessed value is worse than none,
  because the container then starts and looks healthy while running on it.

  A `:?` refusal would abort the render of the WHOLE compose document over one
  unset name, taking down every other service in the deployment — none of which
  asked for that variable. Whether a declared name is actually set is a
  deployment question, answered where the deployment can name it, not by a
  document that refuses to parse. (The templates do use `:?` by hand for the
  handful of variables that are genuinely load-bearing for one service; see the
  archiver recorder's external-IOC `EPICS_CA_NAME_SERVERS`. That is a per-
  variable judgement a generic macro has no way to make.)

  What a bare `${VAR}` leaves behind when nothing sets it is an EMPTY value, not
  an absent one: compose interpolates the empty string, so the container sees
  the variable set-and-empty. That distinction matters to code reading it as
  `e.get(NAME, default)`, for which "" is a value that beats the default — the
  bridges' DEDUP_PATH comments are where it bites. Compose's list form
  (`- VAR`) is the spelling that omits an unset name instead, and it is not
  available here: these `environment:` blocks are mappings throughout, and YAML
  admits no block that is half mapping and half list.

  Empty-when-unset is also what keeps a value rotatable by editing the env chain
  alone: nothing about it is baked into the image or into a default here, so the
  next render carries whatever the chain carries.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHITESPACE CONTRACT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  As in `_network_axis.j2`: the macro emits a LEADING newline and no trailing
  one, and is called alone on its own line with a left-trim marker, as the last
  thing in an `environment:` block:

      {{- hostenv.passthrough(services.qmd) }}

  With nothing declared the macro emits the empty string and the whole line
  disappears, leaving the block byte-for-byte what it was — rather than the
  blank line an `{% if %}`/`{% endif %}` pair would have left.

  Mind the `{{-`: it strips ALL preceding whitespace, so do not place a call
  where a deliberate blank line precedes it.
#}

{#- The declared host variables, one `NAME: ${NAME}` line each, in author order.

    `indent` defaults to the six spaces an `environment:` entry sits at in these
    templates, which is the only place this macro is called from. -#}
{%- macro passthrough(svc, indent=6) -%}
{%- set s = svc if svc else {} -%}
{%- set declared = s.env | default([], true) -%}
{%- set names = declared if declared is sequence and declared is not string and declared is not mapping else [] -%}
{%- set ns = namespace(lines=['']) -%}
{%- for name in names if name is string -%}
{%- set _ = ns.lines.append(' ' * indent ~ name ~ ': ${' ~ name ~ '}') -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- if ns.lines | length > 1 -%}
{{ ns.lines | join('\n') }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
